1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,899
Session four.

2
00:00:01,179 --> 00:00:03,535
nginx, and the code that explains it.

3
00:00:03,815 --> 00:00:09,137
Every design in this session was someone's answer
to a constraint that may no longer exist.

4
00:00:09,417 --> 00:00:12,000
Knowing which constraint tells
you a law from a habit.

5
00:00:12,933 --> 00:00:14,083
First, a correction.

6
00:00:14,363 --> 00:00:19,866
The myth says: Berners-Lee wrote the first server,
called Mosaic, and Mosaic became Apache.

7
00:00:20,146 --> 00:00:20,963
That is wrong.

8
00:00:21,243 --> 00:00:22,944
There were three separate programs.

9
00:00:23,266 --> 00:00:28,650
CERN httpd, by Tim Berners-Lee on a NeXT
machine in nineteen ninety.

10
00:00:28,930 --> 00:00:30,458
That was the first web server.

11
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:33,467
NCSA Mosaic, in nineteen ninety-three.

12
00:00:33,747 --> 00:00:35,504
It was a browser, not a server.

13
00:00:35,784 --> 00:00:38,568
It had inline images and took the web mainstream.

14
00:00:38,900 --> 00:00:43,664
NCSA HTTPd, also nineteen ninety-
three, by Rob McCool.

15
00:00:43,944 --> 00:00:46,586
That was a server, and it invented CGI.

16
00:00:46,933 --> 00:00:50,424
In nineteen ninety-four McCool
left, development stalled,

17
00:00:50,424 --> 00:00:52,752
and webmasters traded patches by mail.

18
00:00:53,099 --> 00:00:56,721
In nineteen ninety-five that patch
set continued as a project.

19
00:00:57,001 --> 00:00:58,035
A patchy server.

20
00:00:58,315 --> 00:00:59,039
Apache.

21
00:00:59,366 --> 00:01:05,888
In nineteen ninety-six, Apache passed NCSA HTTPd,
and stayed number one for twenty years.

22
00:01:06,833 --> 00:01:09,803
Apache then spent a decade trying to outrun fork.

23
00:01:10,133 --> 00:01:12,181
Apache one point three, prefork.

24
00:01:12,461 --> 00:01:15,998
A pool of processes forked at
startup instead of per request.

25
00:01:16,278 --> 00:01:18,416
The setup cost leaves the request path.

26
00:01:18,696 --> 00:01:21,381
But one process still parks on one connection.

27
00:01:21,699 --> 00:01:24,489
Apache two point zero, the worker MPM.

28
00:01:24,769 --> 00:01:28,993
Processes running many threads, with eight
megabytes shared and a stack each.

29
00:01:29,273 --> 00:01:33,672
Linux pthreads in nineteen ninety-nine to two
thousand two were new and buggy.

30
00:01:33,999 --> 00:01:36,638
Apache two point four, the event MPM.

31
00:01:36,918 --> 00:01:39,984
A listener thread parks idle keep-alives on epoll,

32
00:01:39,984 --> 00:01:43,294
and hands a worker a connection
only when it has work.

33
00:01:43,574 --> 00:01:46,304
It is the default on Unix since two point four.

34
00:01:46,633 --> 00:01:51,348
Three architectures, one question: how do I
make the per-connection worker cheaper?

35
00:01:51,628 --> 00:01:53,552
None of them was a different question.

36
00:01:54,499 --> 00:01:57,942
Java's answer was different in
style: stop creating things.

37
00:01:58,266 --> 00:02:03,362
CGI, from nineteen ninety-three, did all
this for every request: fork, exec,

38
00:02:03,362 --> 00:02:06,714
load the interpreter, parse the script, run, exit.

39
00:02:07,033 --> 00:02:09,346
The servlet, from nineteen ninety-seven,

40
00:02:09,346 --> 00:02:12,353
loads the class at startup and
instantiates it once.

41
00:02:12,633 --> 00:02:17,116
Then each request is just a method call:
service, request, response.

42
00:02:17,396 --> 00:02:19,253
The object outlives the request.

43
00:02:19,533 --> 00:02:20,507
That is the trick.

44
00:02:20,833 --> 00:02:26,752
J two EE, in nineteen ninety-nine, made this a
multi-layer app server: a web tier,

45
00:02:26,752 --> 00:02:28,918
an EJB tier, and the database.

46
00:02:29,266 --> 00:02:32,717
Connection pools do the same thing
for the database handshake.

47
00:02:32,997 --> 00:02:36,071
It is the same move as prefork and FastCGI.

48
00:02:36,999 --> 00:02:38,485
Now it is two thousand two.

49
00:02:38,765 --> 00:02:40,982
Here are the numbers everyone was staring at.

50
00:02:41,299 --> 00:02:43,303
Ten thousand concurrent connections.

51
00:02:43,583 --> 00:02:46,683
That is C ten K, Dan Kegel, nineteen ninety-nine.

52
00:02:47,033 --> 00:02:48,857
Eight megabytes of stack per thread.

53
00:02:49,137 --> 00:02:52,486
Ten thousand threads is eighty
gigabytes of address space.

54
00:02:52,833 --> 00:02:56,511
And one thousand twenty-four,
FD_SETSIZE, hard-coded.

55
00:02:56,791 --> 00:02:58,385
A ulimit does not move it.

56
00:02:58,733 --> 00:03:00,806
Everyone is optimising the worker.

57
00:03:01,086 --> 00:03:02,788
Somebody is about to delete it.

58
00:03:03,733 --> 00:03:07,817
Igor Sysoev, at Rambler, the big Russian
portal, in two thousand two.

59
00:03:08,166 --> 00:03:13,459
Rambler's Apache servers were falling over under
connection volume, not request volume.

60
00:03:13,799 --> 00:03:17,647
He started nginx in two thousand
two to solve C ten K.

61
00:03:17,927 --> 00:03:22,101
The first public release was on the fourth
of October, two thousand four,

62
00:03:22,101 --> 00:03:23,644
the anniversary of Sputnik.

63
00:03:23,924 --> 00:03:27,938
Version one point zero came only in
April two thousand eleven.

64
00:03:28,266 --> 00:03:32,746
The goal: serve ten thousand mostly idle
connections from one process,

65
00:03:32,746 --> 00:03:35,818
and let something else generate dynamic content.

66
00:03:36,098 --> 00:03:39,747
That is why nginx is a reverse
proxy, not an app server.

67
00:03:40,066 --> 00:03:42,779
Apache asked: how do I make the worker cheaper?

68
00:03:43,059 --> 00:03:46,555
nginx asked: why is there a worker
per connection at all?

69
00:03:46,835 --> 00:03:50,723
A connection needs a few hundred bytes of
state, and a place in a list,

70
00:03:50,723 --> 00:03:52,366
until it has something to say.

71
00:03:53,299 --> 00:03:54,397
The process model.

72
00:03:54,677 --> 00:03:58,605
One process per core, plus a master that
never touches a connection.

73
00:03:58,933 --> 00:04:03,434
The master binds port eighty, reads the
config, forks, and handles signals.

74
00:04:03,714 --> 00:04:04,586
Nothing else.

75
00:04:04,933 --> 00:04:08,176
Then there is one worker per core,
each with its own event loop.

76
00:04:08,499 --> 00:04:13,710
Plus a cache manager, which evicts the least
recently used entries, and a cache loader,

77
00:04:13,710 --> 00:04:15,027
which warms the index.

78
00:04:15,366 --> 00:04:16,884
You can check this with ps.

79
00:04:17,164 --> 00:04:22,211
The master has parent process one, and the number
of workers does not change under load,

80
00:04:22,211 --> 00:04:24,332
unlike a fork server, where it grows.

81
00:04:24,612 --> 00:04:31,631
The code is ngx_master_process_cycle,
ngx_start_worker_processes, and ngx_spawn_process.

82
00:04:32,566 --> 00:04:34,605
All of nginx, in six lines.

83
00:04:34,885 --> 00:04:37,400
This is ngx_process_events_and_timers.

84
00:04:37,733 --> 00:04:38,992
Find the nearest timer.

85
00:04:39,272 --> 00:04:40,313
Process events.

86
00:04:40,593 --> 00:04:42,426
Process posted accept events.

87
00:04:42,706 --> 00:04:44,301
Release the accept mutex.

88
00:04:44,581 --> 00:04:45,751
Expire timers.

89
00:04:46,031 --> 00:04:48,087
Process the remaining posted events.

90
00:04:48,367 --> 00:04:49,670
That is the whole server.

91
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,816
Notice that the timer is an argument to the sleep.

92
00:04:53,096 --> 00:04:55,568
So nginx never polls for timeouts.

93
00:04:55,848 --> 00:04:58,588
It just sleeps exactly until the next one is due.

94
00:04:58,933 --> 00:05:01,028
And handlers do not run inside the poll.

95
00:05:01,308 --> 00:05:03,499
They are posted, and drained afterwards.

96
00:05:04,433 --> 00:05:06,478
How do the workers share new connections?

97
00:05:06,758 --> 00:05:07,676
With one line.

98
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:13,294
ngx accept disabled equals the total
connection slots divided by eight,

99
00:05:13,294 --> 00:05:14,861
minus the free slots.

100
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:20,019
In plain words: once I am using more than
seven eighths of my connection slots,

101
00:05:20,019 --> 00:05:24,045
stop competing for new connections, and
let my siblings take them.

102
00:05:24,325 --> 00:05:26,681
No coordinator and no shared counter.

103
00:05:26,961 --> 00:05:28,532
Each worker decides alone.

104
00:05:28,866 --> 00:05:33,717
The accept mutex has been off by default since
version one point eleven point three.

105
00:05:34,066 --> 00:05:38,171
The thundering herd, where all workers
wake up for a single connection,

106
00:05:38,171 --> 00:05:39,906
was real in two thousand four.

107
00:05:40,186 --> 00:05:43,657
Now EPOLLEXCLUSIVE, in Linux four
point five and later,

108
00:05:43,657 --> 00:05:46,939
and SO_REUSEPORT let the kernel
fan connections out.

109
00:05:47,866 --> 00:05:52,308
select versus epoll, stated as: O of
watched, versus O of active.

110
00:05:53,333 --> 00:05:54,811
Where does the watch list live?

111
00:05:55,091 --> 00:05:58,009
With select, in your process,
rebuilt on every call.

112
00:05:58,289 --> 00:06:01,630
With epoll, in the kernel, edited with epoll_ctl.

113
00:06:01,966 --> 00:06:07,173
The cost per iteration: select does an O of
n build, an O of n kernel walk,

114
00:06:07,173 --> 00:06:08,492
and an O of n scan.

115
00:06:08,772 --> 00:06:10,705
epoll costs O of ready events.

116
00:06:11,033 --> 00:06:13,330
With ten thousand idle and ten active,

117
00:06:13,330 --> 00:06:16,595
select does thirty thousand units
of work to find ten.

118
00:06:16,875 --> 00:06:17,974
epoll does ten.

119
00:06:18,300 --> 00:06:23,505
The ceiling: FD_SETSIZE of one thousand
twenty-four, fixed at compile time,

120
00:06:23,505 --> 00:06:24,685
versus ulimit -n.

121
00:06:25,033 --> 00:06:26,330
And what does ulimit do?

122
00:06:26,610 --> 00:06:29,822
For select, nothing, because the
limit is a bitmap width.

123
00:06:30,102 --> 00:06:31,534
For epoll, everything.

124
00:06:31,866 --> 00:06:34,902
select is portable everywhere since
nineteen eighty-three.

125
00:06:35,182 --> 00:06:38,404
epoll is Linux only, with kqueue on BSD.

126
00:06:38,733 --> 00:06:42,819
Everything here is downstream of one
decision: who owns the watch list.

127
00:06:43,766 --> 00:06:46,165
Every event loop author writes this bug once.

128
00:06:46,445 --> 00:06:47,409
The stale event.

129
00:06:47,733 --> 00:06:50,052
epoll_wait returns a batch of events.

130
00:06:50,400 --> 00:06:54,344
Handling event one may close the
connection that event seven points at.

131
00:06:54,666 --> 00:06:57,850
A new client then takes that
recycled file descriptor,

132
00:06:57,850 --> 00:07:00,976
and event seven is delivered to
the wrong connection.

133
00:07:01,300 --> 00:07:03,023
nginx's fix is clever.

134
00:07:03,303 --> 00:07:07,420
Structures are aligned so the low bit
of a pointer is always zero,

135
00:07:07,420 --> 00:07:11,101
and that bit is used as an instance
flag, flipped on reuse.

136
00:07:11,381 --> 00:07:12,600
One branch checks it.

137
00:07:12,880 --> 00:07:14,698
A free bit, no extra memory.

138
00:07:15,033 --> 00:07:19,646
The companion repository uses an
explicit generation counter instead,

139
00:07:19,646 --> 00:07:21,250
which is easier to read.

140
00:07:22,200 --> 00:07:23,064
sendfile.

141
00:07:23,344 --> 00:07:24,843
Four copies become two.

142
00:07:28,666 --> 00:07:32,792
With read and write, data goes from disk to
the page cache, to your buffer,

143
00:07:32,792 --> 00:07:35,103
to the socket buffer, to the network card.

144
00:07:35,383 --> 00:07:37,847
Four copies, and two mode switches per chunk.

145
00:07:41,166 --> 00:07:45,620
With sendfile, data goes from disk to the
page cache, to the socket buffer,

146
00:07:45,620 --> 00:07:46,807
to the network card.

147
00:07:47,087 --> 00:07:50,885
Two copies, both by DMA, and no
mode switches per chunk.

148
00:07:51,165 --> 00:07:53,369
The data never enters your process.

149
00:07:53,700 --> 00:07:58,071
The limit: if you must touch the bytes,
for gzip, TLS or templating,

150
00:07:58,071 --> 00:08:00,129
they have to come to user space.

151
00:08:00,409 --> 00:08:03,309
So gzip on and sendfile on fight each other.

152
00:08:03,589 --> 00:08:05,792
That is why kernel TLS exists.

153
00:08:06,133 --> 00:08:09,608
An honest benchmark, sixty-four
megabytes over loopback:

154
00:08:09,608 --> 00:08:14,511
read takes thirty-seven milliseconds and two
thousand forty-eight system calls.

155
00:08:14,791 --> 00:08:17,140
mmap takes twenty-three milliseconds and four.

156
00:08:17,420 --> 00:08:21,047
sendfile takes thirty-three
milliseconds and one system call.

157
00:08:21,327 --> 00:08:26,142
On loopback there is no network card or DMA,
so wall clock is about the same.

158
00:08:26,422 --> 00:08:30,360
The real win is two thousand forty-
eight system calls down to one,

159
00:08:30,360 --> 00:08:32,182
and headroom under concurrency.

160
00:08:32,462 --> 00:08:36,085
A benchmark that does not model the
bottleneck measures the wrong thing.

161
00:08:37,033 --> 00:08:38,886
Why is it called a reverse proxy?

162
00:08:39,166 --> 00:08:40,573
Because proxy was taken.

163
00:08:40,900 --> 00:08:45,547
A forward proxy, like Squid from nineteen
ninety-six, sits next to the clients.

164
00:08:45,827 --> 00:08:48,832
It caches for campus users and
protects your uplink.

165
00:08:49,166 --> 00:08:52,805
A reverse proxy, like nginx, sits
next to the servers.

166
00:08:53,085 --> 00:08:58,602
It caches, routes by path or host, distributes
across upstreams, terminates TLS,

167
00:08:58,602 --> 00:09:01,084
and holds ten thousand slow clients.

168
00:09:01,364 --> 00:09:04,521
The client never configures it, and
usually cannot see it.

169
00:09:04,866 --> 00:09:08,262
The killer feature nobody lists:
it absorbs slow clients.

170
00:09:08,542 --> 00:09:11,174
Your app never has to talk to a phone on two G.

171
00:09:12,100 --> 00:09:13,762
Now the features you configure.

172
00:09:14,042 --> 00:09:15,056
First, the cache.

173
00:09:15,400 --> 00:09:17,327
One directive defines the cache.

174
00:09:18,366 --> 00:09:23,884
keys zone: ten megabytes of shared memory holding
the red-black tree and the LRU queue.

175
00:09:24,164 --> 00:09:25,998
That is the index, not the data.

176
00:09:26,278 --> 00:09:28,383
About eight thousand keys per megabyte.

177
00:09:28,700 --> 00:09:32,106
levels one colon two: two
directory levels on disk,

178
00:09:32,106 --> 00:09:34,979
so no directory ever holds a million files.

179
00:09:35,300 --> 00:09:37,072
max size: the disk ceiling.

180
00:09:37,352 --> 00:09:40,353
The cache manager evicts from the tail of the LRU.

181
00:09:40,700 --> 00:09:46,837
inactive: an item is evicted if it is not read for
sixty seconds, regardless of its TTL.

182
00:09:47,766 --> 00:09:48,827
A class question.

183
00:09:49,107 --> 00:09:52,883
proxy_cache_valid is ten seconds,
inactive is sixty seconds.

184
00:09:53,163 --> 00:09:53,982
What happens?

185
00:09:55,300 --> 00:09:57,295
The content is fresh for ten seconds.

186
00:09:57,575 --> 00:10:00,561
After that it is stale, so nginx revalidates.

187
00:10:00,841 --> 00:10:04,753
But it is kept on disk for up to sixty
seconds since the last access.

188
00:10:05,100 --> 00:10:10,320
And if nobody reads it for sixty seconds, it is
deleted, even if it would still be valid.

189
00:10:10,600 --> 00:10:11,787
Valid is freshness.

190
00:10:12,067 --> 00:10:14,576
Inactive is how long an unread item stays.

191
00:10:15,500 --> 00:10:17,757
Two lines that save you at three in the morning.

192
00:10:18,100 --> 00:10:18,602
One.

193
00:10:18,882 --> 00:10:20,224
proxy_cache_lock on.

194
00:10:20,504 --> 00:10:22,026
This is anti-stampede.

195
00:10:22,306 --> 00:10:27,967
When a popular object expires, only one request
goes to the origin and the others wait.

196
00:10:28,247 --> 00:10:29,537
It is off by default.

197
00:10:29,866 --> 00:10:30,402
Two.

198
00:10:30,682 --> 00:10:32,063
proxy_cache_use_stale.

199
00:10:32,343 --> 00:10:36,957
Serve stale content rather than fail, when
the origin errors, times out,

200
00:10:36,957 --> 00:10:41,315
or returns five hundred, five oh two,
five oh three or five oh four.

201
00:10:41,595 --> 00:10:44,800
The updating option means one
request refreshes the

202
00:10:44,800 --> 00:10:48,256
item while everyone else gets the
stale copy instantly.

203
00:10:48,536 --> 00:10:50,322
That is stale-while-revalidate.

204
00:10:50,666 --> 00:10:54,844
And always add an X-Cache-Status header
with the upstream cache status,

205
00:10:54,844 --> 00:10:56,433
so you can see miss or hit.

206
00:10:57,366 --> 00:10:58,741
location precedence.

207
00:10:59,021 --> 00:11:00,309
It is not file order.

208
00:11:01,333 --> 00:11:03,113
One: equals, exact match.

209
00:11:03,393 --> 00:11:05,695
It wins immediately and the search stops.

210
00:11:06,033 --> 00:11:08,111
Two: caret tilde, a prefix match.

211
00:11:08,391 --> 00:11:11,880
It takes the longest prefix, and
suppresses the regex search.

212
00:11:12,160 --> 00:11:16,106
So/images/a dot php goes to
number two, not the regex.

213
00:11:16,386 --> 00:11:17,922
Half the room gets this wrong.

214
00:11:18,266 --> 00:11:19,516
Three: a regex.

215
00:11:19,796 --> 00:11:22,842
The first match in file order
wins, not the longest.

216
00:11:23,166 --> 00:11:27,461
Four: plain slash, the longest prefix,
used only if no regex matched.

217
00:11:27,800 --> 00:11:33,084
So the algorithm is: exact match first, then the
longest prefix, which is remembered,

218
00:11:33,084 --> 00:11:35,882
and if it is a caret tilde, the search stops.

219
00:11:36,162 --> 00:11:38,660
Then regexes in order, first match wins.

220
00:11:38,940 --> 00:11:40,990
Otherwise use the remembered prefix.

221
00:11:41,933 --> 00:11:46,111
Why are five hundred prefixes free, while
five hundred regexes are not?

222
00:11:46,433 --> 00:11:51,151
Prefixes are stored in a ternary search
tree, built at config parse time,

223
00:11:51,151 --> 00:11:52,896
and walked per URI segment.

224
00:11:53,176 --> 00:11:56,866
Regexes are a plain array, walked in
order on every request.

225
00:11:57,146 --> 00:12:01,810
The data structure is the documentation:
a tree means the longest wins,

226
00:12:01,810 --> 00:12:03,781
an array means the first wins.

227
00:12:04,100 --> 00:12:07,065
And nginx has no dot htaccess, deliberately,

228
00:12:07,065 --> 00:12:11,851
so it never has to stat its way up the
directory tree on every request.

229
00:12:12,799 --> 00:12:16,711
Load balancing across upstreams:
smooth weighted round robin.

230
00:12:16,991 --> 00:12:18,272
This is the core of it.

231
00:12:18,599 --> 00:12:22,335
Each round, every peer's current weight
grows by its effective weight.

232
00:12:22,615 --> 00:12:24,773
Pick the peer with the highest current weight.

233
00:12:25,053 --> 00:12:26,961
Then subtract the total from the winner.

234
00:12:27,999 --> 00:12:30,934
Take weights three and one, so a
is three and b is one.

235
00:12:31,214 --> 00:12:33,994
Round one: add, and the weights are three and one.

236
00:12:34,274 --> 00:12:34,877
Pick a.

237
00:12:35,157 --> 00:12:37,670
Subtract four from a: minus one, one.

238
00:12:37,999 --> 00:12:39,399
Round two: two and two.

239
00:12:39,679 --> 00:12:41,946
A tie, and the first peer wins, so a.

240
00:12:42,226 --> 00:12:43,804
Subtract: minus two, two.

241
00:12:44,133 --> 00:12:45,603
Round three: one and three.

242
00:12:45,883 --> 00:12:46,586
Pick b.

243
00:12:46,866 --> 00:12:48,576
Subtract: one, minus one.

244
00:12:48,899 --> 00:12:50,522
Round four: four and zero.

245
00:12:50,802 --> 00:12:51,406
Pick a.

246
00:12:51,686 --> 00:12:53,333
We are back to zero, zero.

247
00:12:53,666 --> 00:12:59,648
The ratio is the same three to one, but compare
naive weighted round robin, which sends a,

248
00:12:59,648 --> 00:13:01,244
a, a, then b, in bursts.

249
00:13:01,524 --> 00:13:03,399
The smooth version interleaves b.

250
00:13:03,679 --> 00:13:06,109
The lecture writes the sequence as a b a a.

251
00:13:06,389 --> 00:13:09,228
Depending on how ties break, you may see a a b a.

252
00:13:09,508 --> 00:13:12,210
Either way, b is in the middle,
not queued at the end.

253
00:13:12,490 --> 00:13:17,237
Measured over twenty-four requests, six went to
one upstream and eighteen to the other.

254
00:13:17,517 --> 00:13:18,882
Exactly three to one.

255
00:13:19,162 --> 00:13:24,747
The other algorithm to try is least_conn: compare
the p ninety-nine with a slow upstream.

256
00:13:25,666 --> 00:13:27,205
Fallbacks, with try_files.

257
00:13:27,533 --> 00:13:28,627
Three small blocks.

258
00:13:28,966 --> 00:13:30,237
Is this a real file?

259
00:13:30,566 --> 00:13:31,700
A real directory?

260
00:13:32,033 --> 00:13:32,542
No?

261
00:13:32,822 --> 00:13:34,338
Hand the URI to the app.

262
00:13:34,618 --> 00:13:36,444
The last entry is never tested.

263
00:13:36,724 --> 00:13:38,809
It is the fallback, not a candidate.

264
00:13:39,133 --> 00:13:45,024
A named location, with an at sign, is reachable
only from try_files or error_page,

265
00:13:45,024 --> 00:13:46,245
never from a URL.

266
00:13:46,566 --> 00:13:52,154
WordPress, Rails, Django, and every single page
app router are this one directive,

267
00:13:52,154 --> 00:13:54,812
plus an app that reads the request URI.

268
00:13:55,733 --> 00:13:57,023
Now a tour of the source.

269
00:13:57,303 --> 00:13:59,723
Line numbers drift, function names do not.

270
00:14:00,003 --> 00:14:02,981
Since nginx puts the return type on its own line,

271
00:14:02,981 --> 00:14:07,053
grep for the caret and the function
name finds only the definition.

272
00:14:08,099 --> 00:14:14,921
src core is nginx's own standard library: strings,
arrays, lists, hashes, red-black trees,

273
00:14:14,921 --> 00:14:16,664
queues, pools, buffers.

274
00:14:16,999 --> 00:14:21,811
src event holds the loop, and six ways to
wait, chosen at configure time.

275
00:14:22,133 --> 00:14:28,834
src http holds the HTTP state machine, the
phase engine and the upstream framework.

276
00:14:29,166 --> 00:14:31,823
src os unix is the syscall layer.

277
00:14:32,166 --> 00:14:36,630
src stream is the same
architecture for raw TCP and UDP,

278
00:14:36,630 --> 00:14:40,216
which makes nginx a layer four load balancer.

279
00:14:40,566 --> 00:14:45,776
And src mail does the same for IMAP,
POP3 and SMTP proxying.

280
00:14:46,099 --> 00:14:47,583
Two files to read first.

281
00:14:47,863 --> 00:14:53,742
ngx_queue.h is an intrusive doubly linked list:
the link pointers live inside the node,

282
00:14:53,742 --> 00:14:55,499
so there is no allocation.

283
00:14:55,779 --> 00:14:58,830
The cache LRU and the posted event queues use it.

284
00:14:59,166 --> 00:15:01,755
ngx_palloc.h is the memory pool.

285
00:15:02,035 --> 00:15:06,598
Allocate from a pool attached to the request,
and destroy the whole pool at the end.

286
00:15:06,878 --> 00:15:10,789
No reference counting, no class of leaks,
and no free on the hot path.

287
00:15:11,733 --> 00:15:15,146
The HTTP parser is a resumable state machine.

288
00:15:15,466 --> 00:15:18,261
The request line alone has twenty-seven states.

289
00:15:21,599 --> 00:15:24,524
No regex, no strtok, no allocation.

290
00:15:24,804 --> 00:15:26,592
It consumes one byte at a time.

291
00:15:26,933 --> 00:15:28,800
It can stop between any two bytes.

292
00:15:29,080 --> 00:15:32,782
The state is saved, and parsing
resumes where it stopped,

293
00:15:32,782 --> 00:15:36,419
because a request line may arrive
in three TCP segments.

294
00:15:36,766 --> 00:15:41,024
This is what non-blocking costs you:
every parser has to be resumable.

295
00:15:41,966 --> 00:15:44,286
Every request walks through eleven phases.

296
00:15:44,633 --> 00:15:50,001
Post read, server rewrite, find config,
rewrite, post rewrite, pre-access, access,

297
00:15:50,001 --> 00:15:52,815
post access, pre-content, content, and log.

298
00:15:53,133 --> 00:15:55,389
The engine loops over the phase handlers.

299
00:15:55,669 --> 00:16:01,110
And here is the subtle part: when a checker
returns NGX_OK, it does not mean done.

300
00:16:01,390 --> 00:16:02,508
It means suspended.

301
00:16:02,788 --> 00:16:04,960
This is a hand-rolled coroutine in C.

302
00:16:05,240 --> 00:16:08,724
The request resumes at the phase it
left, when its event fires.

303
00:16:09,066 --> 00:16:14,032
Which module runs when: limit_req in pre-
access, auth_basic in access,

304
00:16:14,032 --> 00:16:19,837
try_files in pre-content, and proxy_pass,
fastcgi_pass and static files in content.

305
00:16:20,766 --> 00:16:23,829
Back to FastCGI, this time at the byte level.

306
00:16:24,166 --> 00:16:28,979
Every record has an eight byte header:
version, type, request ID in two bytes,

307
00:16:28,979 --> 00:16:32,990
content length in two bytes, padding
length, and a reserved byte.

308
00:16:33,270 --> 00:16:35,184
Then the content data, then padding.

309
00:16:35,533 --> 00:16:42,574
A begin request header is zero one, zero one, zero
zero, zero one, zero zero, zero eight,

310
00:16:42,574 --> 00:16:44,236
zero zero, zero zero.

311
00:16:44,516 --> 00:16:48,104
Those are the first eight bytes of
every FastCGI connection.

312
00:16:48,384 --> 00:16:53,900
And an empty params record is zero one, zero
four, then the request, then length zero.

313
00:16:54,180 --> 00:16:55,665
That marks the end of params.

314
00:16:55,999 --> 00:16:57,925
An empty record is a delimiter.

315
00:16:58,205 --> 00:17:00,430
Forget it, and the responder waits forever.

316
00:17:00,710 --> 00:17:03,114
That is length plus delimiter, at two levels.

317
00:17:04,033 --> 00:17:09,596
The companion repository is a ladder of small
programs, about twelve hundred lines,

318
00:17:09,596 --> 00:17:12,075
against nginx's two hundred thousand.

319
00:17:12,355 --> 00:17:13,859
The difference is error paths.

320
00:17:14,899 --> 00:17:17,787
Fork per client matches Apache one point three.

321
00:17:18,133 --> 00:17:23,412
A pthread per client matches the Apache two
worker, and prints its eight megabyte stack.

322
00:17:23,733 --> 00:17:27,717
One thread with select dies at
descriptor one thousand twenty-four,

323
00:17:27,717 --> 00:17:29,204
and ulimit does not help.

324
00:17:29,533 --> 00:17:32,093
One thread with epoll matches nginx.

325
00:17:32,373 --> 00:17:35,059
Five thousand connections, and nothing happens.

326
00:17:35,399 --> 00:17:40,752
read versus mmap versus sendfile: two thousand
forty-eight system calls become one.

327
00:17:41,099 --> 00:17:45,089
The FastCGI responder talks the
wire protocol at both ends.

328
00:17:45,433 --> 00:17:50,420
And one nginx conf, with five demos: routing,
cache and weights, and fallbacks.

329
00:17:51,366 --> 00:17:52,852
Four ideas to leave with.

330
00:17:53,199 --> 00:17:53,702
One.

331
00:17:53,982 --> 00:17:55,225
Ask the other question.

332
00:17:55,505 --> 00:17:57,077
Apache made the worker cheaper.

333
00:17:57,357 --> 00:17:59,298
nginx asked why there was one.

334
00:17:59,633 --> 00:18:00,169
Two.

335
00:18:00,449 --> 00:18:02,413
Check whether the constraint still exists.

336
00:18:02,693 --> 00:18:06,559
Thread per connection died at eight
megabytes plus context switches.

337
00:18:06,839 --> 00:18:09,390
Goroutines and virtual threads brought it back.

338
00:18:09,733 --> 00:18:10,319
Three.

339
00:18:10,599 --> 00:18:13,099
The data structure is the documentation.

340
00:18:13,379 --> 00:18:15,769
Prefixes in a tree: longest wins.

341
00:18:16,049 --> 00:18:18,286
Regexes in an array: first wins.

342
00:18:18,633 --> 00:18:19,186
Four.

343
00:18:19,466 --> 00:18:20,639
Amortise the setup.

344
00:18:20,919 --> 00:18:26,136
Prefork, pre-thread, servlets, connection
pools, FastCGI, upstream keepalive.

345
00:18:26,416 --> 00:18:27,769
Six names, one move.

346
00:18:28,099 --> 00:18:30,425
The next question: where did all the state go?

347
00:18:30,705 --> 00:18:34,803
Sessions, sticky routing, TLS
termination, and shared caches.

348
00:18:35,733 --> 00:18:39,134
Here is what the quiz is most likely
to ask from session four.

349
00:19:03,199 --> 00:19:04,364
That is session four.

350
00:19:04,644 --> 00:19:10,107
Next up, session five: the evolution of HTTP,
from nineteen ninety-six to now.

